{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792191168,"nonce":"59nr7aTXC/1pFWXGYc1W262MNU4=","message":"Fixed in 8287782bbd2ff60b88015d5e29204930f2a241d4. Cause: hlp_call_method's closure-runner branches never checked arity, so a dynamic call that left out a trailing parameter (String.indexOf's ?startIndex) handed compiled code a missing argument that hlp_dyn_casti then dereferenced. It was not limited to --mode jit: hybrid crashed the same way once the caller was compiled (a loop of 200k probe(result) calls), and the interpreter read the omitted slot as void, so message.substr(2) through Dynamic returned an empty string there.\n\nNow: hlp_call_method pads trailing parameters that accept null (any pointer kind, so null\u003ci32\u003e included) with null before dispatch; a missing parameter that cannot be null raises HashLink's 'Missing arguments : N expected but M passed' (catchable); the interpreter's execute_hl_function pads the same way. Stock HashLink itself throws 'Missing arguments : 3 expected but 2 passed' for the reporter's indexOf call, so ash is deliberately more lenient for optional parameters, as Haxe's own evaluator is.\n\nNew test optional_dyn_call (indexOf, indexOf with a start, substr with and without a length, lastIndexOf, split; a 20000-iteration hot caller) in interp, hybrid --jit-threshold 1 and jit, against Haxe eval's output; --jit-tier llvm gives the same. The 'Can't cast (i32 (String,null\u003ci32\u003e)) to (i32 (String))' shape from ChimpMonk's larger program was not reproduced here; if it still appears with the workaround removed on a binary built from this commit, it is a separate path and needs that program's trace.","files":null}]}